# (chapter number word, file, heading, plain terms, why paragraph(s), [ch1 sections], [articles])
SPECS = [
("Two","core_02.000_definition_structure.md","Why Definitions Need a Fixed Structure",
 "a word that can mean whatever is convenient protects no one. This chapter exists so that every term in the Constitution can be checked against what actually happens.",
 "A rule is only as strong as the words it uses. If a term like *safety* or *fair treatment* names a goal but says nothing about how to measure it or what must be true in practice, anyone can claim to meet it. This chapter gives every definition the same four parts (what the term is, how it is measured, how it is assessed, and what must hold) and requires all four to be met for the system as it really runs. That is what lets a sentient hold a system to the words it signed up to.",
 ["5","5.2.3","15.2","19.2"],["XVII","XXIV-A"]),
("Three","core_03.000_definition_integrity.md","Why Definition Integrity Exists",
 "a definition can be beaten without breaking it openly, by narrowing it where it is applied or by splitting a harm into pieces too small to count. This chapter exists to close those routes.",
 "Systems rarely say they are ignoring a rule. More often they keep the words and change what they mean in practice: a test is run on a sample that avoids the problem, a harm is split across offices, or a duty is redefined at the point where it applies. This chapter says what counts as failing a definition, including these quieter ways, so that meeting the words while defeating their purpose is still a failure.",
 ["5","15.1","15.1.1","19.2","19.6"],["XIV-C","XVII"]),
("Four","core_04.000_burden_traceability_verification.md","Why the Burden of Proof Exists",
 "the one who claims to comply is the one who must show it. This chapter exists because a sentient harmed by a system rarely has the evidence to prove what went wrong.",
 "The sentient affected by a system usually knows least about how it works, and the operator knows most. If the burden sat with the affected sentient, most failures could never be shown. This chapter places the burden on whoever claims compliance, and requires the proof to be traceable, open to inspection, and checkable by others, even where security limits how or when it is shown. That keeps a compliance claim from being a promise no one can test.",
 ["5","5.2","13.2","16.4"],["XVII","XVII-A","XVII-C","XIV-C"]),
("Seven","core_07.000_charters.md","Why Charters Exist",
 "no one can tell whether a system or institution stayed within its limits unless the limits were written down first. This chapter exists so those limits are public before anyone relies on them.",
 "Every later check (certification, standing, forum review) asks whether a system or institution did what it was allowed and promised to do. Without a published charter, that question has no fixed answer, and the limits can be redrawn after the fact. This chapter requires a System Charter or Institutional Charter at the scale where it matters, says what each must state, and keeps it true over time. It does this without making anyone ask permission to form or to start work.",
 ["7.2","18.1","18.4","19.1.4"],["XI-E","XI-F","XVII","XXIV-A"]),
("Ten","core_10.000_system_alignment_certification_forum_process_outcomes.md","Why Forum Review of Certification Exists",
 "an evaluation is only trustworthy if someone independent reviews it and the result is published. This chapter exists so that no system certifies itself.",
 "Chapter Nine sets how a system is evaluated. An evaluation run or chosen by the system's own operator would invite the outcome the operator wants. This chapter sends the result to independent forums, in a fixed order, with a published outcome and a gate before a high-impact system goes live. Affected sentients can see the decision and challenge it. That is what turns an evaluation into something others can rely on.",
 ["6","17.5","18.3"],["XIV-A","XVII","XVII-B","XXVI-B"]),
("Eleven","core_11.000_system_alignment_certification_records_recertification.md","Why Certification Records and Recertification Exist",
 "a certificate is only as good as the record behind it and the date it was last checked. This chapter exists so certification stays true after the decision.",
 "Systems change after they are certified: new versions ship, owners change, and risks appear. A certificate that is never re-checked becomes a label that outlives its truth. This chapter keeps the outcome on a record that the system does not control, sets when the system must be checked again, and lets sentients challenge a certification that has gone wrong. It also keeps certification apart from standing, so a certificate is never treated as a verdict on anyone's conduct.",
 ["6","10","16.4"],["XIV-A","XVII","XXVII-C","XXIX-A"]),
("Thirteen","core_13.000_institutional_alignment_certification_forum_process_outcomes.md","Why Forum Review of Institutional Certification Exists",
 "an institution must never choose its own reviewer. This chapter exists so institutional certification is decided by forums the institution does not control.",
 "Chapter Twelve sets what an institution is evaluated on. The institution has every reason to want a favourable result, and often the power to shape who reviews it. This chapter sends the evaluation to the same independent forums, in the same order, as system certification, and publishes the outcome. It also states that no outcome decides whether the institution may exist or operate, so certification can never be used to shut down lawful association.",
 ["7.2","18.3","19.3"],["XI-E","XIII-A","XVII","XXVI-B"]),
("Fourteen","core_14.000_institutional_alignment_certification_records_recertification.md","Why Institutional Records and Recertification Exist",
 "an institution's certification must stay true as the institution changes. This chapter exists so the record is held by someone else and re-checked on time.",
 "Institutions merge, split, change owners, and change leaders. A certification that does not follow those changes would let an institution keep a good result after the facts behind it are gone. This chapter keeps the outcome on a record held by an office the institution does not control, sets when the institution is checked again, including when something material changes, and keeps the certification apart from standing.",
 ["16.4","19.6"],["XI-E","XVII","XXVII-C","XXIX-A"]),
("Fifteen","core_15.000_standing_assessment.md","Why Standing Records Exist",
 "before anyone is credited or held to account, the facts must be settled and written down. This chapter exists so that standing rests on what happened, not on rumour, popularity, or a desired result.",
 "A society that credits help and answers harm needs a fair way to establish what actually happened. Without one, recognition goes to the popular and blame to the unpopular, and an allegation does the work of a finding. This chapter opens the standing pipeline by asking only what happened, on verified facts, and keeps help and harm on separate records so one can never erase the other. Rating and consequences come later, in other chapters.",
 ["3.2","5","6.1","13.1"],["XX","XX-A","XXI-A","XXVII-C"]),
("Sixteen","core_16.000_standing_measurement.md","Why Standing Measurement Exists",
 "once the facts are settled, how good or how harmful they were has to be measured the same way for everyone. This chapter exists so like cases get like measures.",
 "Without a shared scale, the same harm could be called minor in one place and grave in another, and the same help ignored or celebrated depending on who gave it. This chapter measures verified contribution and verified violation on separate axes of one common scale, never netted into a single score. That keeps measurement consistent and stops a sentient's good work from being used to excuse harm, or their past harm from erasing their good work.",
 ["3.2","4","13.1","19.2"],["XX-A","XXI","XXV"]),
("Seventeen","core_17.000_standing_integration.md","Why Standing Integration Exists",
 "measuring help and harm only matters if something follows from it. This chapter exists so that what follows is fair, ordered, and never a merged score.",
 "Chapters Fifteen and Sixteen settle what happened and how much it mattered. Something still has to decide what follows, and in what order, or the results drift into informal reputation that no one can contest. This chapter takes those findings as fixed and sends them on in a set order: violation, correction, and prevention first, then contribution. Help and harm stay on separate tracks, and nothing here rewrites the facts.",
 ["3.2","6.1","19.1.2"],["XX","XX-D","XXI-A"]),
("Eighteen","core_18.000_contribution_consequences.md","Why Contribution Consequences Exist",
 "verified good work should count for something, but never as a way to buy past harm. This chapter exists to say what contribution may and may not do.",
 "A system that never recognizes contribution teaches that effort does not matter. A system that lets contribution cancel harm teaches that harm can be paid for. This chapter lets verified contribution support recognition and readiness for gated pathways that are not locked, and says plainly that it never offsets a verified harm, cancels a lock, or opens a blocked pathway.",
 ["3.1.5","3.2","3.2.6"],["XX-C","XXI-B"]),
("Nineteen","core_19.000_violation_consequences.md","Why Violation Consequences Exist",
 "when harm is verified, the first job is to repair it and stop it happening again. This chapter exists so that consequences fix the problem rather than only punish.",
 "Punishment alone leaves the harmed sentient unrepaired and the cause in place. This chapter puts remedy and correction first, then prevention locks where they are needed to stop a repeat, and keeps the cheap step (an apology or statement) from standing in for the expensive one (actual repair). Each consequence is tied to a verified finding and can be contested.",
 ["6.1","13.1.3","13.1.5","17.5"],["XIV-B","XX-D","XXI","XXI-B"]),
("Twenty","core_20.000_final_effect_restoration_enforcement.md","Why Final Effects and Restoration Exist",
 "a sentient must be able to see exactly what is open and closed to them, and how to get back what was closed. This chapter exists so no consequence becomes a hidden, permanent mark.",
 "After the earlier steps, the results have to land somewhere concrete. If they become a general label, they follow a sentient everywhere and cannot be answered. This chapter states the effect one gated pathway at a time, never as a dignity rank or merged score, and gives every lock that is not permanent a real way back. It also requires that the effects and the redress can be delivered by real institutions.",
 ["6.1","5.2","13.2.4"],["XX-B","XXI-B","XIV-B","XXVII-C"]),
("Twenty-One","core_21.000_misconduct_designation.md","Why Anti-Constitutional Misconduct Designation Exists",
 "some of the gravest harm is not to one sentient but to the protections everyone depends on. This chapter exists to name that harm, narrowly and with extra safeguards.",
 "A violation can be serious in its own right and also attack the system of protection itself, for example by capturing an oversight body or hollowing out a right at scale. Treating that as an ordinary violation would miss the wider danger; treating any serious violation that way would be an easy tool for abuse. This chapter opens only for violations already at the highest slots, asks one further question, and adds safeguards because the designation carries more weight.",
 ["15.1","17.5","19.3.1"],["XXI-A","XXI-B","XXVI","XXVII-B"]),
("Twenty-Two","core_22.000_misconduct_pattern_applications.md","Why Named Misconduct Patterns Exist",
 "attacks on constitutional protection tend to repeat in recognizable forms. This chapter exists so those forms are recognized consistently.",
 "Capture, manipulation of evidence, and domination of markets or information recur across settings. Without named patterns, each forum would have to recognize them from scratch, and similar cases would be decided differently. This chapter names the recurring patterns and routes each into Chapter Twenty-One's decision, without adding new criteria or new consequences.",
 ["11.2","13.2.1","19","19.1.2"],["XIII-A","XVI-A","XVII","XXI-A"]),
("Twenty-Three","core_23.000_forum.md","Why Forums Exist",
 "every right needs a place where a sentient can be heard when it is denied. This chapter exists so that place is fair, independent, timely, and actually reachable.",
 "A right that cannot be brought before someone independent is only a hope. Disputes also go wrong in predictable ways: the powerful choose a friendly venue, a case is passed between offices until it lapses, or the body being challenged hears the challenge. This chapter supervises the forums, decides which forum hears a matter by its primary stake, and keeps any body from judging its own case.",
 ["17.4","18.3","13.1.5"],["XII-C","XIV-B","XXVI-B","XXVI-C","XXVII-C"]),
("Twenty-Four","core_24.000_forum_families_escalation_timeliness_support.md","Why Forum Families, Escalation, and Clocks Exist",
 "a forum is only useful if it has the right expertise, can pass hard questions upward, and decides in time. This chapter exists to make sure all three are true.",
 "Different disputes need different expertise, and some questions are too large for the forum that first hears them. Delay can also defeat a right as surely as a wrong decision. This chapter sets the forum families and their fields, the paths by which a matter escalates or a question goes for ruling, the clocks that keep cases moving, and the support forums need to decide well.",
 ["9.2","13.1.5","15.1","16.6"],["XIV-B","XXVI-C","XXVII-C"]),
("Twenty-Seven","core_27.000_governance.md","Why the Constitutional Contract Exists",
 "power to make rules for others must be earned and kept, not simply held. This chapter exists to say what makes governing power legitimate.",
 "Every system of rules rests on someone's authority to make them. If that authority is never tested, it drifts toward whoever holds it and away from those it governs. This chapter states the minimum that must be true for governing power to count as legitimate and for stewardship to count as constitutional, and how that authority stays answerable to the sentients it governs.",
 ["2.1","17.1","18","18.4"],["X-B","XII","XIII-A","XXVI-D"]),
("Twenty-Eight","core_28.000_non_regression.md","Why Non-Regression Exists",
 "a protection that can be quietly removed is not a protection. This chapter exists so that no change can weaken what this Constitution guarantees.",
 "Protections are most often lost not by repeal but by small steps: a narrower definition, a new procedure, a temporary measure, or an emergency label that never ends. This chapter makes any change that weakens the protections the Constitution depends on invalid, however it is made. It is the substantive test every amendment must pass before the procedural tests in Chapter Twenty-Nine are even reached.",
 ["2.1","14","17.5"],["XXVI-A","XXVIII","XXVIII-A"]),
("Twenty-Nine","core_29.000_amendment_ratification.md","Why Amendment Procedure Exists",
 "a change can be right in substance and still fail if it was made in the dark. This chapter exists so every change is published, traceable, and open to challenge before it takes effect.",
 "A constitution that cannot change will fail, and one that can be changed quietly will be captured. This chapter sets how a change is proposed, published, reviewed, adopted, and recorded, with harder review for changes that affect rights and a contest path that actually works. It also sets how a body adopts this Constitution in the first place.",
 ["5","17.7","18.4"],["XII-A","XXVI-B","XXVIII-B","XXIX-A"]),
("Thirty","core_30.000_expansion_supremacy.md","Why Expansion and Supremacy Rules Exist",
 "this Constitution must be able to grow, and it must sit alongside other law without hiding behind it or overriding it in secret. This chapter exists to say how.",
 "New rights and stronger safeguards should be easy to add, so the text can improve as understanding grows. The Constitution also has to work beside other law: where outside law protects more, it should be respected; where outside law is used as an excuse to destroy records or dodge rights, it should not succeed. This chapter sets both rules.",
 ["2.1","14","15.4"],["VI","XXVIII-A"]),
("Thirty-One","core_31.000_incorporation.md","Why Incorporation Exists",
 "an adopter is bound by the exact text it adopted, no more and no less. This chapter exists so everyone can tell which text that is.",
 "Implementation text keeps improving, and adopters adopt it at different times. Without clear rules, an adopter could be held to a draft it never accepted, or escape a duty by pointing to a newer version. This chapter makes implementation text bind only as adopted, pinned to a recorded edition, with a custody chain that shows who held the authoritative text and when it took effect.",
 ["15.4","15.4.3","16.4"],["XVII","XXVIII-B"]),
]
